d invalidate all following blocks as all subsequent hashes would change. Learn more about this page in our Knowledge Base.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions | VibeLeaderboard (https://www.vibeleaderboard.ai/app/d8a7cafb-d59e-470a-9207-e89df4720091)\nciteturn23search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # HISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions ... A signed manifest lists checksums so users can verify files, and the data is released under CC BY 4.0. ... The site says HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry each day and runs each new npm and PyPI MCP package version in a sandbox. ...     Servers that require a key show less or nothing to the anonymous client\n\n← Back to Tools\n\nIndex / tool\n\n# HISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions\n\nhistor.modelmarket.dev\n\nVisit histor.modelmarket.dev\n\nCategory\n    Cybersecurity\n\nRank\n    \n\nNo. 2267Tools index\n\nPrevious survey · No. 2094 · 30 October 2026\n\nPricing\n    Open Source\n\nType\n    TOOL\n\nUse case\n    Security & Identity\n\nDate\n    Published Oct 22, 2026\n\n## About\n\nMonthly snapshots of a public transparency log covering remote MCP servers in the official registry and npm and PyPI MCP packages. The files hold the tool definitions each server served, every recorded change field by field, pattern scan matches, and advisory classifier readings. A signed manifest lists checksums so users can verify files, and the data is released under CC BY 4.0.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe site says HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry each day and runs each new npm and PyPI MCP package version in a sandbox. It presents its pattern matches and classifier flags as leads worth reading, not as proof that a server is malicious.\n\n### Stated on the product site\n\nLicense\n    CC BY 4.0\n\nRelease cadence\n    A ful